---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout 
  title="How Ratings Work in 16-0play | Simulation Engine Explained"
  description="Understand the mathematics and logic behind player ratings, team chemistry, and match simulations in 16-0play."
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12 md:py-20 text-[var(--color-body)] text-base leading-relaxed">
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black text-[var(--color-ink)] tracking-tight mb-8">The 16-0play Rating System</h1>
    
    <p class="text-lg mb-10">
      The 16-0play simulation engine uses a dynamic rating system designed to mirror the complexities of real cricket. Ratings are not static numbers; they interact with team composition, match conditions, and player form to determine the outcome of every match.
    </p>

    <div class="space-y-12">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Base Player Ratings & Star Stacking</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Every player in the database is assigned a base rating between 70 and 99. This rating is derived from their historical performances, peak statistical seasons, and their impact in the specific role they play.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
          <li><strong>90+ (Elite):</strong> Legendary, match-winning icons capable of carrying a team.</li>
          <li><strong>80-89 (Star):</strong> Premium talent necessary for consistent victories.</li>
          <li><strong>70-79 (Role Player):</strong> Reliable squad members who provide vital balance and depth.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-4">
          <strong>Star Stacking Penalty:</strong> The engine penalizes top-heavy teams to encourage balance. Having more than two 92+ rated players incurs a team-wide penalty. Having more than one 95+ rated player incurs an additional heavy penalty. Furthermore, any overall team strength that exceeds 90 receives a 50% diminishing return penalty on the excess points.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Team Chemistry & Rivalries</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          Matching specific players together can either boost your team's performance or create friction:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
          <li><strong>Signature Pairs (+1 Bonus):</strong> Reuniting iconic duos like <em>Virat Kohli & AB de Villiers</em> or <em>MS Dhoni & Suresh Raina</em> provides a chemistry boost to your overall team strength.</li>
          <li><strong>Rivalry Penalty (-1 Penalty):</strong> Pairing players with historic rivalries (e.g. <em>Virat Kohli & Gautam Gambhir</em>) will actively harm your team's synergy.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Identity & Franchise Core Bonuses</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          The engine rewards teams that establish a clear tactical identity or maintain a franchise core:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
          <li><strong>Tactical Identities:</strong> Fielding 7+ Batters/Wicketkeepers grants a <em>Batting-Heavy</em> bonus. Fielding 5+ Bowlers grants a <em>Bowling-Heavy</em> bonus. Fielding 4+ All-Rounders creates an <em>All-Rounder Army</em>.</li>
          <li><strong>Franchise Core:</strong> Selecting 3 or more players from the same original franchise provides a +1 chemistry bonus, and 5 or more players provides a +2 bonus.</li>
          <li><strong>Overseas Quality Limit:</strong> While you are strictly limited to a maximum of 4 overseas players on the field, the engine applies a slight progressive penalty if you field 2 or more overseas players, simulating the challenge of integrating multiple international stars.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Dynamic Form System</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          In 16-0play, a player's base rating is only the starting point. The engine calculates an active <strong>Form Score</strong> (represented by sparklines on player profiles) that adjusts their effective rating up or down for the next match.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-4">
          <li><strong>Hot Streaks:</strong> Consistent high performances boost a player's form, pushing their effective rating past their base maximum. A team on a massive winning streak (e.g. 12+ games) enters "Dynasty Mode", granting massive momentum bonuses.</li>
          <li><strong>Slumps:</strong> Successive poor matches will drop a player's form, making them a liability against elite opponents.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Match Simulation Logic</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">
          When two teams meet, the engine calculates the expected win probability by weighing the adjusted team ratings against each other. 
        </p>
        <p>
          However, cricket is unpredictable. The engine incorporates controlled variance (representing pitch conditions, weather, and pure luck) to ensure that upsets are always possible. Furthermore, tight matches (40-60% win probability) can trigger "Clutch Player" events where individuals single-handedly flip the result. A team with a 95 overall rating can still lose to an 85 overall team if the form, risk profile, and variance fall the wrong way—which is what makes the 16-0 challenge so incredibly difficult.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-4">Reading the strength breakdown</h2>
        <p class="mb-4">The implementation uses the first eleven filled slots. It calculates batting and bowling averages with role-specific weights, then calculates an effective overall from the players, chemistry, identity bonuses, and deductions. After overall adjustments, the difference from the raw rating average is applied to the batting and bowling display values. Those numbers are connected parts of one calculation, not three independent forecasts.</p>
        <p class="mb-4">For star stacking, the selected players are sorted by rating. The first two players rated 92 or higher have no 92-plus deduction; each additional 92-plus player loses two points from their effective contribution. Among ratings of 95 or higher, the first has no additional deduction and every later one loses three more points. A later 95-plus player can receive both deductions because they also meet the 92 threshold. The displayed stacking figure is the total deductions divided by the number of filled players.</p>
        <p class="mb-4">The overseas deduction is one point for two overseas players, two for three, and three for four or more. Standard XI selection is capped at four. Multiplayer can use a host-selected overseas limit of four or five, so its setting is distinct. Four role identities may also contribute: batting-heavy requires seven BAT/WK roles, bowling-heavy requires five pure BOWL roles, and all-rounder army requires four AR/BAT_AR/BOWL_AR roles. A franchise core gives one point at three players and two at five or more.</p>
        <p class="mb-4">The twelve signature pair checks add one point each up to a chemistry cap of five. Four separate rivalry checks subtract one each up to a cap of three. After these inputs are combined, any overall above 90 is reduced by half of its excess. This is a diminishing return, not a hard maximum of 90.</p>
        <p>Form is generated during a game run from recent simulated player performances; it is separate from historical Cricsheet profile statistics. The form code stores up to five performances, classifies a score from 0 to 100, and converts categories to a squad win-probability adjustment capped at plus or minus six percentage points. For the names and composition of the pair rules, see the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/guides/signature-pairs/">signature-pairs guide</a> and the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/guides/ratings-star-stacking-chemistry/">complete calculation guide</a>.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
